    {
      "type": "workedExamples",
      "id": "worked",
      "navLabel": "Worked examples",
      "title": "Worked examples",
      "lede": "Three kinds of question on _y_ = _x_^2, each worked through in full.",
      "groups": [
        {
          "id": "g-substitution",
          "type": "compact",
          "title": "Substitution",
          "lede": "Put a value of _x_ into the rule and read off _y_. Two complete examples — the second is the one people get wrong.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "id": "ex-neg",
              "label": "A negative value",
              "prompt": "Use the rule _y_ = _x_^2 to find _y_ when _x_ = −4.",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "id": "s1",
                  "text": "Write the rule.",
                  "math": "_y_ = _x_^2"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s2",
                  "text": "Substitute the given value. Keep the brackets — the whole of −4 is squared.",
                  "math": "_y_ = (−4)^2"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s3",
                  "text": "Evaluate. A negative multiplied by a negative gives a positive.",
                  "math": "_y_ = −4 × −4 = 16"
                }
              ],
              "answer": "_y_ = 16, so (−4, 16) lies on the graph."
            },
            {
              "id": "ex-frac",
              "label": "A fraction",
              "prompt": "Use the same rule to find _y_ when _x_ = 3/2.",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "id": "s1",
                  "text": "Substitute. Again the whole value is squared, not just the numerator.",
                  "math": "_y_ = (3/2)^2"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s2",
                  "text": "Square the top and the bottom.",
                  "math": "_y_ = 9/4"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s3",
                  "text": "Squaring a value above 1 makes it larger; this one lands just above 2.",
                  "math": "9/4 = 2.25"
                }
              ],
              "answer": "_y_ = 9/4, so (3/2, 9/4) lies on the graph."
            }
          ],
          "relations": [
            {
              "kind": "relations",
              "label": "What both show",
              "items": [
                "Substitution never changes the rule — it only replaces _x_. The brackets are what keep the whole value together, in a negative and in a fraction alike."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "g-solving",
          "type": "visual",
          "title": "Solving for _x_",
          "lede": "Working backwards: the _y_-value is known and the _x_-values have to be found.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "id": "ex-solve",
              "label": "Find _x_ when _y_ = 16",
              "prompt": "Find the value(s) of _x_ for which _y_ = 16.",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "id": "s1",
                  "text": "Substitute the known _y_-value into the rule.",
                  "math": "16 = _x_^2"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s2",
                  "text": "Take the square root of both sides. A positive number has two square roots.",
                  "math": "_x_ = ±4"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s3",
                  "text": "Check both against the rule.",
                  "math": "4^2 = 16 and (−4)^2 = 16"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s4",
                  "text": "Read it off the graph: the line _y_ = 16 crosses the parabola twice, once each side of the axis."
                }
              ],
              "answer": "_x_ = 4 or _x_ = −4.",
              "visual": {
                "parts": [
                  {
                    "kind": "figure",
                    "figure": {
                      "type": "figure",
                      "figure": "graph",
                      "aspect": "stretch",
                      "grid": "shown",
                      "callouts": "hidden",
                      "domain": {
                        "xMin": -6,
                        "xMax": 6,
                        "yMin": -2,
                        "yMax": 22
                      },
                      "objects": [
                        {
                          "type": "function",
                          "f": "x^2",
                          "label": "y = x^2"
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "line",
                          "y": 16,
                          "label": "y = 16"
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "points",
                          "rows": [
                            [
                              "(−4, 16)",
                              -4,
                              16
                            ],
                            [
                              "(4, 16)",
                              4,
                              16
                            ]
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  }
                ]
              }
            }
          ],
          "relations": [
            {
              "kind": "relations",
              "label": "What the graph shows",
              "items": [
                "The horizontal line _y_ = 16 meets the curve twice, once each side of the axis — which is why the algebra produces two answers rather than one."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "g-symmetry",
          "type": "comparison",
          "title": "Symmetry",
          "lede": "Two values of _x_, worked separately, arriving at the same _y_.",
          "examples": [
            {
              "id": "ex-pos",
              "label": "Starting from _x_ = 3",
              "prompt": "Find the point on _y_ = _x_^2 where _x_ = 3.",
              "steps": [
                {
                  "id": "s1",
                  "text": "Substitute the positive value.",
                  "math": "_y_ = 3^2"
                },
                {
                  "id": "s2",
                  "text": "Evaluate.",
